WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.940
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:06.287
Building the Attribute-Driven Migration.

3
00:00:06.287 --> 00:00:10.050
This is Chapter 7: machine
configuration packages.

4
00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:13.524
Learn's cmdlets will build you a
package and a policy definition,

5
00:00:13.524 --> 00:00:17.063
but nothing in them says whether
this is the package you reviewed,

6
00:00:17.063 --> 00:00:19.947
whether it leaks details of
the machine that built it,

7
00:00:19.947 --> 00:00:22.700
or whether it will reach
servers another tool owns.

8
00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:24.587
That check is the pipeline's job.

9
00:00:24.587 --> 00:00:27.881
We'll follow Learn's procedure
and Microsoft's sample,

10
00:00:27.881 --> 00:00:31.999
see what they leave out, then walk
the build, two labs and the dangers.

11
00:00:31.999 --> 00:00:37.500
One note before we begin: the code is
illustrative, tested against fixtures.

12
00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:43.675
Why does a package need
a pipeline around it?

13
00:00:43.675 --> 00:00:47.250
Because three questions go
unanswered by Microsoft's tools.

14
00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:49.850
Is the package going out
the one somebody reviewed?

15
00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:53.917
Does it carry details of the
person and machine that built it,

16
00:00:53.917 --> 00:00:56.113
into a file every server can read?

17
00:00:56.113 --> 00:01:00.512
And will an enforcing policy reach servers
that another tool is supposed to own?

18
00:01:00.512 --> 00:01:04.403
On the Arc track, every setting
has exactly one owning tool,

19
00:01:04.403 --> 00:01:08.537
recorded in the configuration
database and mirrored in a tag.

20
00:01:08.537 --> 00:01:11.343
For the settings machine
configuration owns,

21
00:01:11.343 --> 00:01:16.060
the code is a desired state configuration,
and Azure Policy delivers it.

22
00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:22.210
Here's Learn's model, in three parts.

23
00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:24.723
A package says what the
machine should look like.

24
00:01:24.723 --> 00:01:29.023
A policy definition points at that
package by its address and its hash.

25
00:01:29.023 --> 00:01:34.119
And when the definition is assigned, every
machine in scope gets a guest assignment:

26
00:01:34.119 --> 00:01:37.960
the agent downloads the package,
evaluates it and reports back.

27
00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:41.270
Each assignment is one of
three types: audit only,

28
00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:45.160
apply once and report drift,
or apply and keep correcting.

29
00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:48.173
The spelling is case sensitive,
which will matter later.

30
00:01:48.173 --> 00:01:51.774
Custom content for audit and
apply is generally available,

31
00:01:51.774 --> 00:01:54.640
with minimum agent versions on the slide.

32
00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:00.715
Which cloud matters here in three ways.

33
00:02:00.715 --> 00:02:05.095
The endpoints differ: servers in
Azure Government reach a government

34
00:02:05.095 --> 00:02:09.777
machine configuration host, with regional
endpoints in the US Gov regions.

35
00:02:09.777 --> 00:02:12.372
The way machines read the package matters:

36
00:02:12.372 --> 00:02:15.412
the build uses each machine's
own system identity,

37
00:02:15.412 --> 00:02:20.378
because Learn says a user-assigned identity
doesn't work for Arc-enabled servers.

38
00:02:20.378 --> 00:02:25.525
And cost: for Arc-enabled Windows servers
with the right licensing, it's included,

39
00:02:25.525 --> 00:02:29.424
but that activation isn't offered
in two of the US Gov regions,

40
00:02:29.424 --> 00:02:30.828
so plan to buy it there.

41
00:02:30.828 --> 00:02:34.180
This estate's servers
report to commercial Azure.

42
00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:40.266
Learn documents the whole procedure,

43
00:02:40.266 --> 00:02:43.355
one page per step, and the
build follows it exactly.

44
00:02:43.355 --> 00:02:45.554
You set up an authoring environment,

45
00:02:45.554 --> 00:02:48.705
write a desired state
configuration and compile it.

46
00:02:48.705 --> 00:02:52.480
You package it, and test it with
the same agent the servers run.

47
00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:55.815
You publish the package to
storage, optionally sign it,

48
00:02:55.815 --> 00:02:59.818
and generate a policy definition
that records the package's hash.

49
00:02:59.818 --> 00:03:02.106
Then you publish the definition to Azure.

50
00:03:02.106 --> 00:03:05.618
That's eight steps and eight
commands, all on the slide.

51
00:03:05.618 --> 00:03:07.418
The build replaces none of them.

52
00:03:07.418 --> 00:03:09.450
What it adds goes between them.

53
00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.012
Learn is clear about three
things you must not do.

54
00:03:16.012 --> 00:03:17.937
Don't put secrets in a package.

55
00:03:17.937 --> 00:03:19.937
Don't exceed a hundred megabytes.

56
00:03:19.937 --> 00:03:22.083
And when you release a new version,

57
00:03:22.083 --> 00:03:27.218
give the package a new name but keep the
same policy identifier, raise the version,

58
00:03:27.218 --> 00:03:32.199
and let the hash be recomputed; change
only the address and the agent rejects it.

59
00:03:32.199 --> 00:03:37.487
The repository also pins Microsoft's
sample project, with hashes a test checks.

60
00:03:37.487 --> 00:03:40.926
It's five years older than
the module the chapter uses,

61
00:03:40.926 --> 00:03:44.912
and two things in it no longer
hold, including a renamed cmdlet.

62
00:03:44.912 --> 00:03:47.510
Its compiled file still packages.

63
00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:54.505
Running Learn's commands offline
shows what they don't do,

64
00:03:54.505 --> 00:03:57.473
and the first set of gaps is
about the package itself.

65
00:03:57.473 --> 00:04:00.964
The compiled file records who
built it, on which machine,

66
00:04:00.964 --> 00:04:04.911
and where, and that file goes to
storage the whole fleet can read.

67
00:04:04.911 --> 00:04:09.151
Compiling twice gives different
bytes, and so does packaging twice,

68
00:04:09.151 --> 00:04:12.104
so a reviewer can't tell
a real change from noise,

69
00:04:12.104 --> 00:04:15.285
and a rebuilt package won't
match the reviewed hash.

70
00:04:15.285 --> 00:04:17.085
Nothing scans for secrets.

71
00:04:17.085 --> 00:04:18.873
And module versions float.

72
00:04:18.873 --> 00:04:22.710
Each gap has a fix in the
example, pinned by a test.

73
00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:29.547
The second set of gaps is
in the policy definition.

74
00:04:29.547 --> 00:04:33.913
The cmdlet accepts whatever identifier
and version you give it, so a fresh

75
00:04:33.913 --> 00:04:38.636
identifier quietly creates a second
definition instead of updating the first.

76
00:04:38.636 --> 00:04:43.872
An enforcing definition reaches every
machine in scope, whoever owns the setting.

77
00:04:43.872 --> 00:04:48.635
The switch that includes Arc-enabled servers
defaults to off, and nothing tells you.

78
00:04:48.635 --> 00:04:51.885
And Learn's storage token
example lasts three years.

79
00:04:51.885 --> 00:04:55.449
The verdict is simple: Learn's
procedure is the procedure,

80
00:04:55.449 --> 00:04:57.497
and the build replaces none of it.

81
00:04:57.497 --> 00:05:01.100
It cleans, checks and
records in between the steps.

82
00:05:04.850 --> 00:05:07.298
Running the current
module also turned up a

83
00:05:07.298 --> 00:05:09.813
few places where it
differs from Learn's text.

84
00:05:09.813 --> 00:05:12.164
The file names, a
parameter's shape and the

85
00:05:12.164 --> 00:05:15.138
package contents aren't
quite what Learn describes.

86
00:05:15.138 --> 00:05:19.500
The package works either way, and the
test pins what the module actually does.

87
00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:21.000
The bigger one is a role.

88
00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:25.550
An enforcing definition grants its
remediation identity a role named

89
00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:30.100
only by an identifier that Learn's
built-in roles reference doesn't list.

90
00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:32.625
Microsoft's own role tables name it.

91
00:05:32.625 --> 00:05:34.064
So before relying on it,

92
00:05:34.064 --> 00:05:38.220
read the role in a test subscription
and record what it can do.

93
00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:43.508
So what does the example add?

94
00:05:43.508 --> 00:05:46.530
One configuration, which
audits that old protocol

95
00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:49.482
versions are explicitly
switched off on servers.

96
00:05:49.482 --> 00:05:54.263
It's audit only, and a server with no
explicit setting counts as non-compliant,

97
00:05:54.263 --> 00:05:58.057
on purpose, because the baseline
wants the setting on record.

98
00:05:58.057 --> 00:06:02.257
A module of checks, and a build script
that runs the whole thing offline.

99
00:06:02.257 --> 00:06:04.598
The heart of it is the release registry:

100
00:06:04.598 --> 00:06:07.232
the reviewed record of
what's in production.

101
00:06:07.232 --> 00:06:11.872
It fixes the policy identifier forever,
the mode, the published version,

102
00:06:11.872 --> 00:06:16.470
the identity, and the owner tag that
keeps other tools' servers out.

103
00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:22.582
The first thing the
build adds is a cleaner.

104
00:06:22.582 --> 00:06:26.053
It rewrites the compiled file
into one canonical form:

105
00:06:26.053 --> 00:06:29.985
it strips out who built it and
when, keeps source references in

106
00:06:29.985 --> 00:06:34.071
the short form Microsoft's own
sample uses, and sorts everything.

107
00:06:34.071 --> 00:06:38.658
Compile three times and you get three
different files, but one cleaned file.

108
00:06:38.658 --> 00:06:43.770
A test confirms the cleaned file means the
same thing to the desired state engine.

109
00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:48.552
Then the package check opens the zip
in memory and applies seven rules,

110
00:06:48.552 --> 00:06:52.700
from its layout and size to
secrets and module versions.

111
00:06:56.450 --> 00:06:59.155
Next the build checks
the definition against

112
00:06:59.155 --> 00:07:01.788
the package and the
registry, with nine rules.

113
00:07:01.788 --> 00:07:06.757
The one that catches the most is the hash
check: an enforcing definition carries

114
00:07:06.757 --> 00:07:11.501
the package hash in four places, and
every copy must match this exact package.

115
00:07:11.501 --> 00:07:15.450
So a hand edit or a rebuild
fails here, not on a server.

116
00:07:15.450 --> 00:07:17.713
Then the build writes a provenance record.

117
00:07:17.713 --> 00:07:19.799
Notice there are two kinds of hash.

118
00:07:19.799 --> 00:07:25.075
The cleaned file's hash is reproducible,
so a reviewer can rebuild and match it.

119
00:07:25.075 --> 00:07:28.090
The package hash identifies
the bytes that shipped,

120
00:07:28.090 --> 00:07:31.520
so the security officer can
match the published file.

121
00:07:35.270 --> 00:07:36.258
Who's this for?

122
00:07:36.258 --> 00:07:40.917
The developers and platform engineers who
write server baselines for the Arc track,

123
00:07:40.917 --> 00:07:44.045
and the engineers and security
officers who review them.

124
00:07:44.045 --> 00:07:48.066
You'll want to be comfortable in
PowerShell and know what a desired state

125
00:07:48.066 --> 00:07:52.020
configuration is; the chapter covers
the parts of Azure Policy you need.

126
00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:53.982
The roles are few and narrow.

127
00:07:53.982 --> 00:07:57.544
Publishing a definition needs
the policy contributor role.

128
00:07:57.544 --> 00:08:00.812
Machines read the package
with their own identity

129
00:08:00.812 --> 00:08:03.457
and a read-only storage
role, never a token.

130
00:08:03.457 --> 00:08:07.920
And the remediation role is the one
to confirm before you rely on it.

131
00:08:11.670 --> 00:08:17.370
Lab 1 runs on a Windows laptop and never
signs in, uploads or installs the agent.

132
00:08:17.370 --> 00:08:20.932
You save the pinned modules to a
folder rather than installing them.

133
00:08:20.932 --> 00:08:23.964
Then you follow Learn's
steps by hand: compile once,

134
00:08:23.964 --> 00:08:27.957
package twice, and you get two
different hashes from the same file.

135
00:08:27.957 --> 00:08:31.431
Open the compiled file and
you'll find your own user name,

136
00:08:31.431 --> 00:08:33.319
computer name and folder path.

137
00:08:33.319 --> 00:08:35.108
The checks catch exactly that.

138
00:08:35.108 --> 00:08:40.028
Then you run Microsoft's old sample with
the current module: it still packages,

139
00:08:40.028 --> 00:08:44.700
its cmdlet has been renamed, and
its published file is already clean.

140
00:08:48.450 --> 00:08:49.650
Now the checked build.

141
00:08:49.650 --> 00:08:52.892
It runs offline, passes,
and gives one reminder:

142
00:08:52.892 --> 00:08:56.475
the switch for Arc-enabled
servers defaults to off.

143
00:08:56.475 --> 00:09:00.025
Build it a second time and compare
the two provenance records.

144
00:09:00.025 --> 00:09:04.538
The cleaned file's hash matches across
builds; the package hashes don't.

145
00:09:04.538 --> 00:09:08.574
That's exactly why you publish the
bytes you checked, and never rebuild.

146
00:09:08.574 --> 00:09:13.467
Then break it on purpose: reuse a version,
mistype the mode's capital letter,

147
00:09:13.467 --> 00:09:18.038
or enforce without the owner tag, and
each one fails for its own reason.

148
00:09:18.038 --> 00:09:21.080
Run the tests, and sixty-five pass.

149
00:09:24.830 --> 00:09:28.447
Lab 2 needs a test subscription
and a server you can throw away,

150
00:09:28.447 --> 00:09:31.567
because local testing changes
the machine it runs on.

151
00:09:31.567 --> 00:09:34.013
First you test the package on that server:

152
00:09:34.013 --> 00:09:38.042
it should fail on a default build
and pass once both settings are off.

153
00:09:38.042 --> 00:09:40.992
That's what proves the agent
accepts the cleaned file.

154
00:09:40.992 --> 00:09:46.380
Then you upload the exact package from Lab
1, never a rebuild, and check its hash.

155
00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:48.605
Machines read it with their own identity.

156
00:09:48.605 --> 00:09:52.514
Publish, assign with Arc servers
included, read the result,

157
00:09:52.514 --> 00:09:57.020
and back it all out, including
any leftover guest assignments.

158
00:10:00.770 --> 00:10:04.995
Every danger here has a guard, and
they cluster around a few ideas.

159
00:10:04.995 --> 00:10:08.207
One owning tool per setting,
enforced by a tag filter

160
00:10:08.207 --> 00:10:11.270
at build time and checked
again after assignment.

161
00:10:11.270 --> 00:10:16.358
Never rebuild at release time: publish the
bytes you checked, and match their hash.

162
00:10:16.358 --> 00:10:19.707
Keep build-host details and
secrets out of the package.

163
00:10:19.707 --> 00:10:23.670
Use the machine's own identity
rather than a long-lived token.

164
00:10:23.670 --> 00:10:26.270
Remember the Arc switch is off by default.

165
00:10:26.270 --> 00:10:30.062
And if signing is required,
sign first, then generate the

166
00:10:30.062 --> 00:10:34.790
definition from the signed file,
so the hash is the one that ships.

167
00:10:38.540 --> 00:10:39.890
Here's what done looks like.

168
00:10:39.890 --> 00:10:44.515
The configuration and its registry
entry arrive in the same pull request.

169
00:10:44.515 --> 00:10:47.440
The build used the pinned
modules and found nothing.

170
00:10:47.440 --> 00:10:51.378
Anyone can rebuild the commit and
match the cleaned file's hash.

171
00:10:51.378 --> 00:10:53.838
The package was tested
on a throwaway server,

172
00:10:53.838 --> 00:10:58.227
and the published file's hash matches
the record and the definition everywhere.

173
00:10:58.227 --> 00:11:02.679
No long-lived token, the owner tag
filter on anything that enforces,

174
00:11:02.679 --> 00:11:07.615
Arc servers included where they belong,
and the records filed as evidence.

175
00:11:07.615 --> 00:11:10.677
Next, Chapter 8 puts Ansible beside Arc.

176
00:11:10.677 --> 00:11:11.830
Thanks for watching.
